  booktitle={2026 10th International Conference on I-SMAC (IoT in Social, Mobile, Analytics and Cloud) (I-SMAC)}, 
  title={PAQO: Predictive Agentic Quality-of-Service Optimization for Heterogeneous Enterprise Storage}, 
  year={2026},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1706-1711},
  abstract={Enterprise storage controllers usually react after latency or queue-depth thresholds have been crossed. That control model is insufficient when VMware virtual machines, Hyper-V guests, Kubernetes persistent volumes, databases, Kafka brokers, Spark stages, snapshots, and backup jobs share the same controller, cache, media, or replication domain. This paper specifies PAQO, a predictive agentic quality-of-service controller that converts heterogeneous telemetry into a common topology-aware state, forecasts P95 and P99 latency together with queue and cache pressure, and proposes proactive policy changes before application degradation is observed. The control problem is formulated as a constrained partially observable Markov decision process. Tail-latency exceedance, queue saturation, migration cost, fairness, and action churn are represented explicitly in the reward and cost functions. A deterministic safety supervisor validates every learned action, applies platform-specific masks, enforces rate and capacity limits, and records rollback state. A typed Python reference implementation includes asynchronous collectors for vSphere, Hyper-V, and Kubernetes, a deep Q-network policy, and dry-run executors for IOPS limits, storage migration, backup rescheduling, and persistent-volume policy changes. A reproducible controlled evaluation across 100 independent randomized traces shows that forecast-guided control reduced nominal mean P99 latency by 29.5% relative to static QoS and by 23.1% relative to reactive thresholds. P99 SLO violation intervals decreased by 93.9% and 81.4%, respectively. These measurements establish feasibility within the stated simulation model; they are not presented as production-array results.},
  keywords={Modeling;Quality of service;Arrays;Forecasting;Learning (artificial intelligence);Printing;Optimization;Limiting;Telemetry;Resource management;agentic infrastructure;storage quality of service;tail latency;reinforcement learning;workload forecasting;VMware;Hyper-V;Kubernetes CSI;distributed storage;data engineering},
